# Copy this file to .env. Every setting has a working default except the model's API key and
# the database, which this file names for development.
# This file is for development on one machine. A deployment sets ENVIRONMENT=production and
# passes its settings through the process environment or a secret manager: the app then never
# copies this file into its environment, so the model keys below must arrive as real
# environment variables. In production the app also refuses to start with development's
# conveniences, and says which: the compose database's password, LOCAL_ACCOUNT=true, no
# STORAGE_URL, no APP_URL, no SMTP_URL, or no key for the model; and it serves no /docs. A
# staging deployment is production too.
# ENVIRONMENT=development

# The AI model writes the summaries and facts, reads scans and images, and answers in the Ask
# tab. Leave the key empty to run without those: parsing, outlines and search still work.
# The default model is Gemini; create a key at https://aistudio.google.com/apikey
GOOGLE_API_KEY=

# Models, written provider:model. Indexing is the high-volume side; the agent answers in Ask.
# AGENT_MODEL=google:gemini-2.5-flash-lite
# INDEX_MODEL=google:gemini-2.5-flash-lite
# As of October 2026 Google serves Gemini 2.5 only to accounts that have used it before. If
# your key is refused, use google:gemini-3.5-flash-lite or reach 2.5 through OpenRouter.
#
# To switch, name another model and add the key its provider reads, for example:
#   google:gemini-3.5-flash-lite               GOOGLE_API_KEY
#   anthropic:claude-opus-5-5                  ANTHROPIC_API_KEY
#   openrouter:google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite    OPENROUTER_API_KEY (any model OpenRouter lists)
# The two models can come from different providers. Others that Pydantic AI supports follow the
# same pattern: https://pydantic.dev/docs/ai/models/overview/
#
# How hard the model thinks: minimal | low | medium | high | xhigh. Set it empty to leave the
# model's own default. Models that cannot think ignore it.
# AGENT_EFFORT=medium
# INDEX_EFFORT=low
#
# What the models cost, for the cost on every usage row and the figure under each answer and
# document: model=in/out per million tokens, separated by commas, with the models named as
# above, in PRICE_CURRENCY (an ISO 4217 code). Empty, no cost is recorded. Embeddings are
# counted in texts, not tokens, and carry no cost.
# MODEL_PRICES=google:gemini-2.5-flash-lite=0.10/0.40
# PRICE_CURRENCY=USD

# Embeddings for semantic search: any OpenAI-compatible /embeddings endpoint.
# The default is a local Ollama (run `ollama pull nomic-embed-text` once).
# Changing the model re-embeds every document on the next start. Changing the size needs
# `uv run python -m app.maintenance rebuild-embeddings` (in backend/, app stopped): the app
# refuses to start until the vector column has been resized, and says so.
# EMBEDDING_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:11434/v1
# EMBEDDING_API_KEY=
# EMBEDDING_MODEL=nomic-embed-text
# EMBEDDING_DIM=768
# EMBEDDING_QUERY_PREFIX="search_query: "
# EMBEDDING_DOCUMENT_PREFIX="search_document: "

# Database. The app has no default: a process without DATABASE_URL stops and says so. This
# is the container docker-compose.yml runs, published on 127.0.0.1 only, with the password
# that file sets; a deployment runs its own database and sets DATABASE_URL in the
# environment. POSTGRES_PORT is the host port the container is published on; change the
# two together.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql+psycopg://emudoc:emudoc@127.0.0.1:5437/emudoc
# POSTGRES_PORT=5437

# Text search configuration written into each passage as it is indexed. Changing it affects
# documents indexed afterwards; re-run the structure stage to re-index older ones.
# FTS_LANGUAGE=english
# The worker's threads: this many documents indexed at once, and this many questions answered at
# once, in lanes of their own so that a backlog of uploads never holds an answer up. An answer's
# thread is idle while the model thinks, so the answers cost memory and a database connection
# each more than anything else; raise them together with the pool below.
# WORKER_CONCURRENCY=2
# ANSWER_CONCURRENCY=8
# Whether this process runs the background worker (indexing, housekeeping) as well. Unset, it
# does in development, so that one `uvicorn` does everything, and does not in production, where
# the deployment runs `uv run python -m app.worker` (in backend/) as its own process and scales
# it apart from the API; without any worker, uploads stay queued. true runs both in one process
# on a small deployment; false in development tries the worker as its own process.
# RUN_WORKER=
# The database connections this process keeps: DB_POOL_SIZE open at all times, and up to
# DB_MAX_OVERFLOW more while those are all in use. A connection is held from a session's first
# statement to its commit, so the API needs about one per request in flight, and a worker one
# per document and per answer it runs at once (an answer hands its back while the model thinks)
# plus three for its heartbeat, housekeeper and alerter; a process that runs the worker refuses
# to start with a pool that cannot hold them. The worker's listener holds one more connection
# outside the pool. Keep the sum over every replica under the database's max_connections
# (Postgres allows 100). When every connection is in use, a request waits DB_POOL_TIMEOUT
# seconds for one and is then refused with 503 and asked to try again, rather than hanging.
# DB_POOL_SIZE=5
# DB_MAX_OVERFLOW=10
# DB_POOL_TIMEOUT=5
# The questions one organisation may have waiting for or receiving an answer at once. The worker
# answers ANSWER_CONCURRENCY of them at a time, shared between the organisations, so the cap
# bounds how long a question waits without letting one organisation keep the others waiting;
# the question past it is refused with 429 and asked to wait a moment. It is the same for every
# organisation; a cap that differs by plan is an entitlement.
# MAX_ANSWERS=20
# MAX_UPLOAD_MB=100
# Where uploaded files and rendered page images are kept: a directory on this machine, or an
# S3-compatible bucket with an optional prefix. The default is backend/data inside the checkout,
# for development; a deployment must name a place of its own. A bucket is reached with the
# standard AWS variables, which the S3 library reads from the environment (in development they
# are copied from this file like the model keys): AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID, AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY,
# AWS_DEFAULT_REGION (the bucket's region; URLs are signed for it) and, for MinIO, Cloudflare
# R2, Backblaze B2 or another compatible service, AWS_ENDPOINT_URL. The bucket stays private:
# files are served through the API, which redirects to URLs signed for 15 minutes.
# `docker compose --profile s3 up -d --wait` runs a local bucket to try this with (see
# docker-compose.yml for its settings).
# STORAGE_URL=file:///var/lib/emudoc
# STORAGE_URL=s3://my-bucket/emudoc
# AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# AWS_DEFAULT_REGION=eu-central-1
# AWS_ENDPOINT_URL=
# How long a rendered page image or thumbnail is kept after it was rendered, in days. They are
# rendered from the original the first time they are asked for; every few hours the backend
# removes the ones older than this (`uv run python -m app.maintenance sweep-page-images` in
# backend/ does it at once), and a page asked for again is rendered again. 0 keeps them for good.
# PAGE_IMAGE_DAYS=30
# Limits on parsing an upload. The parse stage runs the parser in a child process of the worker,
# so that a file that takes too long or too much memory stops that process alone: the seconds
# a parse may take, the memory (address space) it may hold, and the most pages a PDF and rows
# one sheet may have. A file over a limit fails with a message that names the limit, and is not
# retried. 0 removes a limit. A worker runs WORKER_CONCURRENCY parses at once, each with its
# own memory on top of the worker's.
# PARSE_TIMEOUT=300
# PARSE_MEMORY_MB=2048
# PARSE_MAX_PAGES=500
# PARSE_MAX_ROWS=100000

# Accounts and sign-in. Requests carry a session cookie; signing up makes a user and an
# organisation of their own. For development on one machine, LOCAL_ACCOUNT=true lets a request
# without a session act as a local owner account, so no sign-in is needed; never set it where
# the app is reachable by others.
# LOCAL_ACCOUNT=false
# Where the app is reached, for the links in emails and the OAuth redirect URI. Empty takes the
# origin of the request, which is right in development.
# APP_URL=https://emudoc.example.com
# SESSION_DAYS=30
#
# Verification and password-reset mail. Empty writes the messages to the backend log, where the
# links can be followed in development. Otherwise an SMTP relay: smtp:// uses STARTTLS (587),
# smtps:// uses TLS (465).
# SMTP_URL=smtp://user:password@smtp.example.com:587
# MAIL_FROM="Emudoc <no-reply@example.com>"
#
# Where the worker reports the jobs it gave up on: a document lost to a dying worker three
# times, a crash in the pipeline, or a stage that kept failing through every retry. With an
# address set, the worker mails the new failures there every quarter of an hour as one digest.
# Empty sends nothing; `uv run python -m app.maintenance failed-jobs` (in backend/) lists them
# either way, and runs them again with --requeue.
# ALERT_EMAIL=
#
# Sign in with Google or Microsoft. Register an OAuth client with each provider, with the
# redirect URI {APP_URL}/api/auth/oauth/google/callback (or .../microsoft/callback), and put
# its id and secret here. A provider without an id is not offered.
# Google: https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials (a Web application client).
# GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
# GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=
# Microsoft: an app registration in Entra ID with a web redirect URI. "common" accepts work,
# school and personal accounts; a tenant id restricts sign-in to that directory. Add the
# optional claim xms_edov to the ID token so that verified addresses are recognised as such.
# MICROSOFT_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
# MICROSOFT_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=
# MICROSOFT_OAUTH_TENANT=common
